### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to merge two sorted lists into one sorted result. This tests Stream.concat, sorted, and awareness of the efficiency trade-off.

### How do you merge with concat and sorted?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class MergeSortedLists {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> branch1 = List.of(100, 300, 500, 700);
        List<Integer> branch2 = List.of(200, 400, 600, 800);

        List<Integer> merged = Stream.concat(
                branch1.stream(), branch2.stream())
            .sorted()
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Merged: " + merged);
    }
}
```

```text
Merged: [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800]
```

### What is the efficiency trade-off?

| Approach | Time | Exploits pre-sort |
|---|---|---|
| concat + sorted | O((m+n) log(m+n)) | No |
| Two-pointer merge | O(m+n) | Yes |

```java
// Optimal two-pointer merge
public static List<Integer> merge(
        List<Integer> a, List<Integer> b) {
    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>(a.size() + b.size());
    int i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
        if (a.get(i) <= b.get(j)) {
            result.add(a.get(i++));
        } else {
            result.add(b.get(j++));
        }
    }
    while (i < a.size()) result.add(a.get(i++));
    while (j < b.size()) result.add(b.get(j++));
    return result;
}
```

The two-pointer merge is linear and leverages the fact that both inputs are already sorted.

### How do you remove duplicates from the merged result?

```java
List<Integer> branch1 = List.of(100, 300, 500);
List<Integer> branch2 = List.of(300, 500, 700);

List<Integer> mergedDistinct = Stream.concat(
        branch1.stream(), branch2.stream())
    .distinct()
    .sorted()
    .toList();

System.out.println("Merged distinct: " + mergedDistinct);
```

```text
Merged distinct: [100, 300, 500, 700]
```

### How do you merge with a custom comparator?

```java
List<String> codes1 = List.of("CUR", "FD", "SAV");
List<String> codes2 = List.of("HDFC", "ICICI", "SBI");

List<String> merged = Stream.concat(
        codes1.stream(), codes2.stream())
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)
        .thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
    .toList();

System.out.println("Merged by length: " + merged);
```

### How do you merge more than two lists?

```java
List<List<Integer>> allBranches = List.of(
    List.of(100, 400), List.of(200, 500), List.of(300, 600));

List<Integer> merged = allBranches.stream()
    .flatMap(Collection::stream)
    .sorted()
    .toList();

System.out.println("All merged: " + merged);
```

```text
All merged: [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600]
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: forgetting sorted() and assuming concat preserves sort order.

```java
// WRONG: concat does not merge-sort; it just appends
List<Integer> wrong = Stream.concat(
        List.of(100, 300).stream(),
        List.of(200, 400).stream())
    .toList();
System.out.println(wrong);  // [100, 300, 200, 400] NOT sorted!

// CORRECT: add sorted()
List<Integer> correct = Stream.concat(
        List.of(100, 300).stream(),
        List.of(200, 400).stream())
    .sorted()
    .toList();
System.out.println(correct);  // [100, 200, 300, 400]
```

Mistake: modifying the input lists.

```java
// WRONG: addAll modifies the first list
branch1.addAll(branch2);  // UnsupportedOperationException on immutable list!

// CORRECT: concat creates a new stream without modifying inputs
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the time complexity?" O((m+n) log(m+n)) for concat+sorted; O(m+n) for two-pointer.
- "How do you merge K sorted lists efficiently?" Use a priority queue (min-heap) of K entries.
- "Can you do this in parallel?" Yes, sorted() handles parallel internally, but two-pointer is inherently sequential.
